From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 15:41:35 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] ext4: do not warn when the extent status cache cannot grow

ext4_find_extent() caches the extents it reads through
ext4_es_cache_extent(), which inserts them into the inode's extent status
tree under i_es_lock with GFP_ATOMIC. The insert is a cache fill: the
caller ignores its result, and a missing entry only means the extent tree
is read again the next time. When the allocation fails the page
allocator nevertheless dumps a page allocation failure with a stack, as it
does for every atomic request that misses the fast path at the minimum
watermark:

  page allocation failure: order:0, mode:0x40830(GFP_ATOMIC|__GFP_RECLAIMABLE|__GFP_COMP)
   new_slab
   kmem_cache_alloc_noprof
   __es_insert_extent
   ext4_es_cache_extent
   ext4_cache_extents
   ext4_find_extent
   ext4_get_verity_descriptor
   fsverity_get_descriptor
   __fsverity_file_open

The other atomic allocation of this kind is the first attempt of
ext4_es_insert_extent() and ext4_es_remove_extent(), which run without a
preallocated extent and, when the allocation fails, retry with extents
allocated under __GFP_NOFAIL. Add __GFP_NOWARN to the one allocation
they share, as the mballoc group index already is: every caller handles
its failure.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 fs/ext4/extents_status.c | 9 ++++++++-
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/fs/ext4/extents_status.c b/fs/ext4/extents_status.c
index 31dc0496f8d0cd6033296710cdeea2c339da5542..77b44e3369bd9565567fecf25b4a86d927287620 100644
--- a/fs/ext4/extents_status.c
+++ b/fs/ext4/extents_status.c
@@ -510,8 +510,15 @@ static inline bool ext4_es_must_keep(struct extent_status *es)
 
 static inline struct extent_status *__es_alloc_extent(bool nofail)
 {
+	/*
+	 * Every caller handles the failure: a cache fill is dropped and the
+	 * extent tree is read again later, and the first attempt of an insert
+	 * or removal retries with an extent preallocated under __GFP_NOFAIL.
+	 * It needs no warning.
+	 */
 	if (!nofail)
-		return kmem_cache_alloc(ext4_es_cachep, GFP_ATOMIC);
+		return kmem_cache_alloc(ext4_es_cachep,
+					GFP_ATOMIC | __GFP_NOWARN);
 
 	return kmem_cache_zalloc(ext4_es_cachep, GFP_KERNEL | __GFP_NOFAIL);
 }
